11 mod3, odd (correct)\nn=14:14\u22612 mod3, even (correct)\nn=15:15\u22610 mod3, odd (correct)\n\nYes, this holds. So we can conjecture that for all n, a_n is even if and only if n \u22612 mod3, otherwise it's odd.\n\nIf this is true, then the product a_{n-2} * a_{n-1} would have the following parities:\n\nLet's take n\u22653. Then n-2 ranges from 1 upwards.\n\nIf n \u22610 mod3, then n-2 \u22610-2=1 mod3. So a_{n-2} is odd (since n-2\u22611 mod3). a_{n-1} is n-1 \u22612 mod3, so even. So product is odd*even=even.\n\nIf n \u22611 mod3, then n-2\u22611-2=2 mod3. a_{n-2} is even (n-2\u22612 mod3). a_{n-1} is n-1\u22610 mod3, which is odd. Product is even*odd=even.\n\nIf n \u22612 mod3, then n-2\u22610 mod3. a_{n-2} is odd. a_{n-1} is n-1\u22611 mod3, odd. Product is odd*odd=odd.\n\nSo for n \u22610 or1 mod3, a_{n-2}*a_{n-1} is even, and for n\u22612 mod3, it's odd. Therefore, in the recurrence, for n\u22653:\n\n- If n \u22610 or1 mod3, use the first recurrence:5a_{n-1}-3a_{n-2}\n\n- If n\u22612 mod3, use the second recurrence:a_{n-1}-a_{n-2}\n\nThis seems to be the case based on our earlier calculations. For example:\n\nn=3:3\u22610 mod3, used first recurrence.\n\nn=4:4\u22611 mod3, used first recurrence.\n\nn=5:5\u22612 mod3, used second recurrence.\n\nn=6:6\u22610 mod3, used first recurrence.\n\nn=7:7\u22611 mod3, used first recurrence.\n\nn=8:8\u22612 mod3, used second recurrence.\n\nEtc.\n\nSo the rule is indeed based on n mod3. Now, given this, perhaps the key to proving that a_n\u22600 is to show that given the recurrence relations and the parity structure, the terms can never reach zero.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can use induction, leveraging the parity patterns. Let's try to formalize the parity observations.\n\nFirst, let's establish by induction that for all n\u22651:\n\n- If n \u22612 mod3, then a_n is even.\n\n- Otherwise, a_n is odd.\n\nBase cases:\n\nn=1:1\u22611 mod3, odd. Correct.\n\nn=2:2\u22612 mod3, even. Correct.\n\nn=3:3\u22610 mod3, odd (a3=7).