understanding what each of these expressions looks like. \n\nFirst, notice that each expression is of the form \\(x^3 y - x y^3\\). Let me factor that. Taking \\(x^3 y - x y^3\\), I can factor out an \\(xy\\), which gives \\(xy(x^2 - y^2)\\). Then, \\(x^2 - y^2\\) factors further into \\((x - y)(x + y)\\). So each expression simplifies to \\(xy(x - y)(x + y)\\). That might be helpful.\n\nSo, for example, the first expression \\(a^3 b - ab^3\\) becomes \\(ab(a - b)(a + b)\\). Similarly for the others. Now, we need to show that at least one of these three factored expressions is divisible by 10. \n\nDivisibility by 10 requires that the number is divisible by both 2 and 5. So, for each expression, we need to check if it has at least one factor of 2 and one factor of 5. If we can show that for at least one of the three expressions, both 2 and 5 divide it, then we're done. \n\nTherefore, perhaps we can approach this by considering the parity (mod 2) and modulo 5 properties of a, b, c. Since a, b, c are distinct positive integers, their residues mod 2 and mod 5 must be considered. Maybe by the pigeonhole principle, certain residues must occur among the three numbers, leading to the conclusion that at least one expression is divisible by 10.\n\nLet me break this down into two parts: divisibility by 2 and divisibility by 5. Then, we can combine the results.\n\nFirst, divisibility by 2. For an expression \\(xy(x - y)(x + y)\\) to be even, at least one of the factors must be even. Let's see:\n\n- If either x or y is even, then the product xy is even, so the entire expression is even.\n- If x and y are both odd, then (x - y) and (x + y) are both even, since odd minus odd is even, and odd plus odd is even. Thus, (x - y)(x + y) would be divisible by 4. Therefore, even if x and y are both odd, the product will still be divisible by 4, hence even.\n\nTherefore, regardless of the parity of x and y, the expression \\(xy(x - y)(x + y)\\) is always even. So each of the three expressio