t positive integers, but their residues mod 5 don't necessarily have to be distinct. For example, a could be 1, b could be 6, c could be 11, all congruent to 1 mod 5, but they are distinct integers. However, in such a case, if all three are congruent to the same residue mod 5, then in each expression, say, a - b would be 1 - 1 = 0 mod 5, so that expression would be divisible by 5. Wait, but if all three numbers are congruent to the same residue mod 5, then all pairwise differences would be 0 mod 5, so all three expressions would be divisible by 5. But in that case, since they are distinct integers, even if they are congruent mod 5, their differences would be multiples of 5. But wait, if a \u2261 b \u2261 c mod 5, then a - b \u2261 0 mod 5, b - c \u2261 0 mod 5, etc. So, then in each expression, the (x - y) term would be 0 mod 5, hence each expression would be divisible by 5. But in that case, since all three expressions are divisible by 5 and 2, all three would be divisible by 10, so certainly at least one is. So, perhaps in the case where two or more numbers share the same residue mod 5, then the difference terms would lead to divisibility by 5.\n\nAlternatively, if all three residues are distinct mod 5. But since there are three numbers and four possible non-zero residues (1,2,3,4), by the pigeonhole principle, they can all be distinct. For example, residues 1,2,3. But in such a case, how can we ensure that one of the expressions is divisible by 5.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps using Fermat's little theorem. Since 5 is prime, for any integer x not divisible by 5, x^4 \u2261 1 mod 5. Therefore, x^4 - 1 \u2261 0 mod 5. So, x^4 \u2261 1 mod 5. Therefore, x^2 \u2261 \u00b11 mod 5. Wait, but x^2 mod 5 can be 1, 4, 4, 1 for x =1,2,3,4. So, squares mod 5 are 1 or -1 (since 4 \u2261 -1 mod 5). Therefore, perhaps x^2 - y^2 \u2261 (x - y)(x + y) mod 5. If either x \u2261 y mod 5 or x \u2261 -y mod 5, then x^2 - y^2 \u2261 0 mod 5.\n\nTherefore, in the expression \\(xy(x - y)(x 