ne triangle, is it possible that one of the medians is equal to half of a side? Let's check with the example earlier. In triangle ABC with sides 5,6,7, the median from A was sqrt(28) \u22485.2915, which is not equal to BM=3. Similarly, the median from B would be different. Let's compute the median from B to AC.\n\nUsing the formula: m_b = (2a\u00b2 + 2c\u00b2 - b\u00b2)/4. For median from B to AC: sides a=5, c=7, b=6.\n\nWait, no. Wait, in the original triangle ABC, sides opposite to A, B, C are BC=6, AC=7, AB=5. So the median from B is to AC. The formula would be m_b = (2AB\u00b2 + 2BC\u00b2 - AC\u00b2)/4.\n\nWait, maybe I need to recall the correct formula. The median from vertex B to side AC is given by m_b = (2AB\u00b2 + 2BC\u00b2 - AC\u00b2)/4. So plugging in the values:\n\n2*(5\u00b2) + 2*(6\u00b2) -7\u00b2 all over 4.\n\n2*25 + 2*36 -49 =50 +72 -49=73. 73/4=18.25, so m_b= sqrt(18.25)\u22484.272. Which is not equal to half of AC, which is 3.5. So in this case, the median from B is \u22484.272, not equal to 3.5. So AM, BM, etc., in the original triangle don't equal half-sides.\n\nTherefore, in this example, cutting any median would not produce an isosceles triangle. But maybe in some other scalene triangle? Suppose we construct a scalene triangle where, say, the median from A is equal to AB. Is that possible?\n\nLet me attempt to construct such a triangle. Let\u2019s suppose we have triangle ABC, scalene, with AB = median from A. Let\u2019s denote the median from A to BC as AM. We want AM = AB.\n\nUsing the formula for the median: AM\u00b2 = (2AB\u00b2 + 2AC\u00b2 - BC\u00b2)/4.\n\nSet AM = AB, so:\n\nAB\u00b2 = (2AB\u00b2 + 2AC\u00b2 - BC\u00b2)/4\n\nMultiply both sides by 4:\n\n4AB\u00b2 = 2AB\u00b2 + 2AC\u00b2 - BC\u00b2\n\nSubtract 2AB\u00b2:\n\n2AB\u00b2 = 2AC\u00b2 - BC\u00b2\n\nDivide both sides by 2:\n\nAB\u00b2 = AC\u00b2 - (BC\u00b2)/2\n\nSo BC\u00b2 = 2(AC\u00b2 - AB\u00b2)\n\nSo BC = sqrt(2(AC\u00b2 - AB\u00b2))\n\nThis requires that AC > AB, and 2(