gree \u22652, leading to an infinite sequence of required edges, which is impossible in a finite graph. Therefore, our assumption that there are no degree 1 vertices must be false. Hence, there must exist at least one vertex of degree 1.\n\nThis seems like a valid argument. By assuming no degree 1 vertices, we force an infinite chain of required edges, which is impossible. Therefore, there must be a vertex of degree 1.\n\nTherefore, the answer is that such a student must exist, proven by contradiction using the pigeonhole principle and the finiteness of the graph.", "deepseek_solution": "To address the problem, we model the students and their acquaintances as a graph where vertices represent students and edges denote mutual acquaintances. The key condition is that any two vertices (students) with the same degree (number of acquaintances) are non-adjacent. We aim to prove there exists a vertex of degree 1.\n\n**Proof by Contradiction:**\n\n1. **Assumption:** Suppose no student knows exactly one other student. Thus, all vertices have degree 0 or \u22652.\n\n2. **Existence of Maximum Degree Vertex:** Let \\( v \\) be a vertex with maximum degree \\( \\Delta \\geq 2 \\). Vertex \\( v \\) has \\( \\Delta \\) neighbors.\n\n3. **Neighbors' Degrees:** Each neighbor of \\( v \\) has degree \u22652 (since degree 1 is excluded by assumption). The possible degrees for these neighbors are \\( 2, 3, \\ldots, \\Delta-1 \\).\n\n4. **Pigeonhole Principle:** There are \\( \\Delta \\) neighbors and \\( \\Delta - 2 \\) possible distinct degrees. By the pigeonhole principle, at least two neighbors, say \\( u \\) and \\( w \\), share the same degree \\( d \\geq 2 \\).\n\n5. **Non-Adjacent Neighbors:** Since \\( u \\) and \\( w \\) have the same degree, they cannot be adjacent. Each must connect to \\( d-1 \\) vertices other than \\( v \\).\n\n6. **Infinite Regress:** The vertices connected to \\( u \\) and \\( w \\) (other than \\( v \\)) must themselves have degrees \u22652, requiring further connections. This creates an infinite chain of necessary edges, which is impossible in a finite graph.\n\n**Conclusion:** The assumption leads to a contradiction. Therefore, there must exist at least one vertex of degree 1.\n\n**Final Answer:**  \nThere must exist a student who knows exactly one other student.  \n\\boxed{\\text{Such a student exists.}}", "ground_truth_solution": "\nNous chercherons \u00e0 prouver qu'il existe un \u00e9l\u00e8ve qui conna\u00eet exactement un seul autre \u00e9l\u00e8ve. Pour ce faire, nous utiliserons les propri\u00e9t\u00e9s et hypoth\u00e8ses donn\u00e9es dans le probl\u00e8me.\n\n1. **Hypoth\u00e8se de Base:**\n   Soit $A$ l'\u00e9l\u00e8ve qui conna\u00eet le plus de personnes. Appelons $k$ le nombre de personnes que $A$ conna\u00eet. Donc, $A$ conna\u00eet pr\u00e9cis\u00e9ment $k$ autres \u00e9l\u00e8ves.\n\n2. **Analysons les connaissances des \u00e9l\u00e8ves:**\n   Chaque personne qui conna\u00eet $A$ conna\u00eet entre $1$ et $k$ personnes. Cependant, selon l'hypoth\u00e8se du probl\u00e8me, si deux \u00e9l\u00e8ves connaissent le m\u00eame nombre de personnes, alors ils ne peuvent pas avoir des connaissances en commun. Cela signifie que si un \u00e9l\u00e8ve $B$ conna\u00eet $i$ personnes et un autre \u00e9l\u00e8ve $C$ conna\u00eet $i$ personnes, alors $B$ et $C$ n'ont aucune connaissance en commun. Dans ce cas de figure, deux personnes connaissant exactement $k$ personnes ne peuvent pas se conna\u00eetre.\n\n3. **Arrivons \u00e0 une contradiction:**\n   Si chaque personne connaissait $A$, alors les autres personnes ne peuvent conna\u00eetre le m\u00eame nombre d'amis qu'elles ont en commun avec $A$. Donc, parmi ces personnes, pour tout $i$ entre $1$ et $k$, il existe une personne qui conna\u00eet exactement $i$ personnes. Cette propri\u00e9t\u00e9 doit \u00eatre vraie pour garantir l'unicit\u00e9 de la connaissance partag\u00e9e.\n\n4. **Preuve par l'Absurd:**\n   Supposons qu\u2019il n\u2019existe pas d\u2019\u00