 the limits change from \\( 0 \\) to \\( \\pi \\):\n\n\\[\nL = 2a \\int_{0}^{2\\pi} \\sin\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right) \\, dt = 2a \\cdot 2 \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sin u \\, du\n\\]\n\nEvaluating the integral:\n\n\\[\n4a \\left[ -\\cos u \\right]_{0}^{\\pi} = 4a \\left( -\\cos \\pi + \\cos 0 \\right) = 4a \\left( 1 + 1 \\right) = 8a\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the length of the arc of the cycloid is \\(\\boxed{8a}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo find the length of one arch of the cycloid given by the parametric equations \n\\[ x = a(t - \\sin t) \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y = a(1 - \\cos t), \\]\nwe need to evaluate the arc length integral. \n\n1. The variable \\( t \\) changes from \\( 0 \\) to \\( 2\\pi \\) for one full arc of the cycloid.\n\n2. Begin by computing the derivatives \\( x'_t \\) and \\( y'_t \\):\n   \\[ x'_t = \\frac{dx}{dt} = a(1 - \\cos t), \\]\n   \\[ y'_t = \\frac{dy}{dt} = a \\sin t. \\]\n\n3. Next, use these derivatives to form the expression under the square root in the arc length formula:\n   \\[ \\sqrt{\\left( x'_t \\right)^2 + \\left( y'_t \\right)^2}. \\]\n\n4. Substitute the derivatives:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\left( x'_t \\right)^2 + \\left( y'_t \\right)^2 &= \\left( a(1 - \\cos t) \\right)^2 + \\left( a \\sin t \\right)^2 \\\\\n   &= a^2 \\left( 1 - \\cos t \\right)^2 + a^2 \\sin^2 t.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n5. Simplify the expression:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\left( 1 - \\cos t \\right)^2 + \\sin^2 t &= 1 - 2 \\cos t + \\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t \\\\\n   &= 1 - 2 \\cos t + 1 \\quad (\\text{since } \\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t = 1) \\\\\n   &= 2(1 - \\cos t).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n6. The arc length expression becomes:\n   \\[\n   \\left( x'_t \\right)^2 + \\left( y'_t \\right)^2 = 2a^2(1 - \\cos t).\n   \\]\n\n7. We can use the trigonometric identity \\( 1 - \\cos t = 2 \\sin^2 \\left( \\frac{t}{2} \\right) \\):\n   \\[\n   2a^2(1 - \\cos t) = 2a^2 \\cdot 2 \\sin^2 \\left( \\frac{t}{2} \\right) = 4a^2 \\sin^2 \\left( \\frac{t}{2} \\right).\n   \\]\n\n8. Hence, we have:\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{\\left( x'_t \\right)^2 + \\left( y'_t \\right)^2} = \\sqrt{4a^2 \\sin^2 \\left( \\frac{t}{2} \\right)} = 2a \\sin \\left( \\frac{t}{2} \\right).\n   \\]\n\n9. The arc length formula is then:\n   \\[\n   L = \\int_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\sqrt{\\left( x'_t \\right)^2 + \\left( y'_t \\right)^2} \\, dt = \\int_{0}^{2 \\pi} 2a \\sin \\left( \\frac{t}{2} \\right) \\, dt.\n   \\]\n\n10. Change the variable \\( u = \\frac{t}{2} \\), hence \\( du = \\frac{1}{2} dt \\). Then \\( t = 0 \\rightarrow u = 0 \\) and \\( t = 2\\pi \\rightarrow u = \\pi \\):\n    \\[\n    \\int_{0}^{2 \\pi} 2a \\sin \\left( \\frac{t}{2} \\right) \\, dt = 4a \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sin u \\, du.\n    \\]\n\n11. Calculate the integral:\n    \\[\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    4a \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sin u \\, du &= 4a \\left. ( -\\cos u ) \\right|_{0}^{\\pi} \\\\\n    &= 4a \\left[ -\\cos \\pi - ( -\\cos 0 ) \\right] \\\\\n    &= 4a \\left[ -(-1) - ( -1 ) \\right] \\\\\n    &= 4a ( 1 + 1 ) = 8a.\n    \\end{aligned}\n    \\]\n\n12. Therefore, the length of one arch of the cycloid is:\n    \\[\\boxed{8a}.\\]\n", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let cube \\( ABCD-A_1B_1C_1D_1 \\) have an edge length of 1. Point \\( M \\) lies on the ray \\( AA_1 \\) and point \\( N \\) lies on the ray \\( BC \\). Given that segment \\( MN \\) intersects edge \\( C_1D_1 \\), find the minimum length of segment \\( MN \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Alright, let me try to tackle this geometry problem. So, we have a cube ABCD-A\u2081B\u2081C\u2081D\u2081 with edge length 1. Points M and N are on the rays AA\u2081 and BC respectively, and the segment MN intersects edge C\u2081D\u2081. We need to find the minimum length of MN. Hmm, okay. Let me start by visualizing the cube and the points mentioned.\n\nFirst, let me recall the cube structure. The cube has edges of length 1, so all edges are 1 unit long. Let's name the v