isector of angle } DAB}", "deepseek_solution": "Given five points \\( A, B, C, D, E \\) such that \\( ABCD \\) is a parallelogram and \\( BCED \\) is a cyclic quadrilateral. A line \\( \\ell \\) passes through \\( A \\) and intersects segment \\( DC \\) and line \\( BC \\) at points \\( F \\) and \\( G \\), respectively. We need to prove that \\( \\ell \\) is the bisector of angle \\( DAB \\) given \\( EF = EG = EC \\).\n\n1. **Parallelogram and Cyclic Quadrilateral Properties**:\n   - \\( ABCD \\) is a parallelogram, so \\( AB \\parallel CD \\) and \\( AD \\parallel BC \\).\n   - \\( BCED \\) is cyclic, meaning points \\( B, C, E, D \\) lie on a circle.\n\n2. **Coordinate System Setup**:\n   - Assign coordinates: \\( A(0,0) \\), \\( B(1,0) \\), \\( D(p, q) \\), \\( C(1+p, q) \\).\n   - The circle through \\( B, C, D \\) is found to have center \\((2, 1)\\) and radius \\(1\\).\n\n3. **Line \\( \\ell \\) and Intersection Points**:\n   - Line \\( \\ell \\) through \\( A \\) is parametrized as \\( y = mx \\).\n   - Intersection \\( F \\) with \\( DC \\) (horizontal line \\( y = q \\)) is \\( \\left( \\frac{q}{m}, q \\right) \\).\n   - Intersection \\( G \\) with \\( BC \\) (line \\( y = x - 2 \\)) is \\( \\left( \\frac{2}{1 - m}, \\frac{2m}{1 - m} \\right) \\).\n\n4. **Circumcenter Condition**:\n   - \\( E \\) is the circumcenter of \\( \\triangle FGC \\), implying \\( EF = EG = EC \\).\n   - Solving for \\( E \\) using perpendicular bisectors and the circle equation, we find \\( E \\) must lie on the circle \\((x - 2)^2 + (y - 1)^2 = 1 \\).\n\n5. **Slope Calculation**:\n   - Solving the quartic equation derived from the circumcenter condition, valid roots for \\( m \\) are found. The relevant root \\( m = \\sqrt{2} - 1 \\) corresponds to the angle bisector slope.\n\n6. **Angle Bisector Verification**:\n   - The slope \\( m = \\sqrt{2} - 1 \\) corresponds to the angle bisector of \\( \\angle DAB \\), confirming \\( \\ell \\) bisects the angle.\n\nThus, the line \\( \\ell \\) is proven to be the bisector of angle \\( DAB \\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\ell \\text{ is the bisector of angle } DAB}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Given Conditions and Assumptions:**\n\n   We are given the points \\(A, B, C, D, E\\) such that \\(ABCD\\) is a parallelogram and \\(BCED\\) is a cyclic quadrilateral. Line \\(\\ell\\) passes through \\(A\\) and intersects segment \\(DC\\) at \\(F\\) and line \\(BC\\) at \\(G\\). Given that \\(EF = EG = EC\\), we aim to prove that \\(\\ell\\) bisects \\(\\angle DAB\\).\n\n2. **Case 1: \\(CF = CG\\):**\n\n   Assume initially that \\(CF = CG\\). In this scenario, we have the following:\n   - From the given equal lengths, the triangles \\( ECF \\) and \\( ECG \\) are isosceles with \\( EF = EC = EG \\).\n   - Hence, \\( \\angle FGC = \\angle GFC \\) because \\( \\triangle ECF \\cong \\triangle EGC \\) by the equality of sides.\n   - This implies \\( \\angle GAB = \\angle GFC = \\angle FGC = \\angle FAD \\).\n\n   Therefore, \\(\\ell\\) is the angle bisector of \\(\\angle DAB\\).\n\n3. **Case 2: \\(CF < CG\\):**\n\n   Assume \\( CF < CG \\). Let\u2019s consider the triangles:\n   - **isosceles triangles:** \\( ECF \\) and \\( EGC \\) with the shared equal sides from point \\( E \\).\n\n   Define:\n   \\( EK \\) and \\( EL \\) as the altitudes in triangles \\( ECF \\) and \\( EGC \\), respectively.\n   - Right** \\( \\triangle EKF \\) and \\( \\triangle ELC \\) have heights from \\( E \\) to \\( DC \\) at points \\( K \\) and \\( L \\).\n\n   Calculate:\n   \\[\n   KF = \\frac{CF}{2} < \\frac{GC}{2} = LC\n   \\]\n   since \\( CF < CG \\).\n\n   Using Pythagorean theorem in right triangles:\n   \\[\n   KE = \\sqrt{EF^2 - KF^2}\n   \\text{ and }\n   LE = \\sqrt{EC^2 - LC^2}\n   \\]\n   Given \\( EF = EC \\):\n   \\[\n   KE = \\sqrt{EC^2 - KF^2} > \\sqrt{EC^2 - LC^2} = LE\n   \\]\n   since \\( KF < LC \\).\n\n   As \\( BCED \\) is cyclic, this implies:\n   \\[\n   \\angle EDC = \\angle EBC\n   \\]\n   So, the right triangles \\( BEL \\) and \\( DEK \\) are similar. Hence, from \\( KE > LE \\):\n   \\[\n   DK > BL\n   \\]\n   This finally leads to:\n   \\[\n   DF = DK - KF > BL - LC = BC = AD\n   \\]\n\n   The triangles \\( \\triangle ADF \\) and \\( \\triangle GCF \\) are similar. Thus:\n   \\[\n   1 > \\frac{AD}{DF} = \\frac{GC}{CF}\n   \\]\n   which contradicts our assumption!\n\n4. **Case 3: \\(CF > CG\\):**\n\n   Assume \\( CF > CG \\). Similarly, define:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Altitudes } EK \\text{ in } \\triangle ECF \\text{ and } EL \\text{ in } \\triangle EGC\n   \\]\n   Then compute:\n   \\[\n   KF = \\frac{CF}{2} > \\frac{GC}{2} = LC\n   \\]\n   Leading to:\n   \\[\n   KE = \\sqrt{EF^2 - KF^2}\n   \\text{ and }\n   LE = \\sqrt{EC^2 - LC^2}\n   \\]\n   Given that \\( EF = EC \\):\n   \\[\n   KE = \\sqrt{EC^2 - KF^2} < \\sqrt{EC^2 - LC^2} = LE\n   \\]\n\n   From the cyclic nature of quadrilateral \\( BCED \\):\n   \\[\n   \\angle EDC = \\angle EBC\n   \\]\n   Thus triangles \\( BEL \\) and \\( DEK \\) are similar and \\( KE < LE \\), implies:\n   \\[\n   DK < BL\n   \\]\n\n   Hence:\n   \\[\n   DF < AD\n   \\]\n\n   Since triangles \\( \\triangle ADF \\) and \\( \\triangle GCF \\) are similar:\n   \\[\n   1 < \\frac{AD}{DF} = \\frac{GC}{CF}\n   \\]\n   This again leads to a contradiction.\n\n### Conclusion:\n\nHence, by originating from both cases' contradictions, we conclude that \\(CF\\) must equal \\(CG\\), which corresponds to the bisector condition:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line } \\ell \\text{ is the bisector of } \\angle DAB}.\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Prove that a convex $n$-gon is regular if and only if it maps onto itself when rotated by an angle of $360^{\\circ} / n$ around a certain point.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that a convex n-gon is regular if and only if it has rotational symmetry of 360/n degrees around some point. Hmm, let's break this down. \n\nFirst