ll 34 +34=68 trees from the two largest types, that's 68 trees of two types. Therefore, a subset of 68 trees can be just two types. Therefore, 68 is possible. Then, 69 would require at least three types. So, N=69. Therefore, the answer is 69.\n\nBut wait, let's check another distribution. Suppose instead we have one type with 52 and the rest with 16 each. Then, the two largest types would be 52 and 16. So, the maximum two types would be 52 +16=68. Wait, same as before. So, same result. So, regardless of how we distribute the 36 extra trees, the maximum two-type total is 68. Therefore, N=69.\n\nWait, but is this correct? Let me check again. If we have one type with 52 and others with 16, then the two largest types are 52 and 16. So, total two types: 52 +16=68. So, maximum two-type total is still 68, so same conclusion. Therefore, regardless of how the 36 extra trees are distributed (whether all to one type, split between two, etc.), the maximum two-type total is 68, hence N=69.\n\nBut let's think if there's another way to distribute the 36 trees such that two types could have more than 68 combined. Suppose we have three types with 17 each and one type with 49. Wait, 17*3=51, 51 +49=100. Then, the two largest types would be 49 and 17=66. That's less than 68. So, worse. If we distribute the 36 as 18, 18, 0, 0. Then, two types would have 16 +18=34 each, and the other two 16 each. So, same as before. So, 34 +34=68. So, same result.\n\nAlternatively, if we spread the 36 more evenly. For example, adding 9 to each type: 16 +9=25 each. Then, the two largest types would be 25 +25=50. Which is less than 68. Therefore, in that case, the maximum two-type total is 50. Then, N would be 51. But this contradicts our earlier conclusion. However, in this case, the problem is that if we spread the 36 more evenly, then the maximum two-type total decreases, but the initial condition of ensuring that any 85 trees include all four types is still satisfied. Wait, but does this distribution 