nal equation as \\( P(x, y) \\).\n\n2. To demonstrate that \\( f \\) is injective, we will assume that \\( f(a) = f(b) \\) for some \\( a, b \\in \\mathbb{R} \\).\n\n3. Substitute \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) into \\( P(x, y) \\) as follows:\n   \\[\n   P(x, a): \\quad f(2f(x)) = f(x - f(a)) + f(x) + a\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   P(x, b): \\quad f(2f(x)) = f(x - f(b)) + f(x) + b\n   \\]\n\n4. By comparing the results of \\( P(x, a) \\) and \\( P(x, b) \\), we get:\n   \\[\n   f(x - f(a)) + f(x) + a = f(x - f(b)) + f(x) + b\n   \\]\n\n5. Simplify the above equation:\n   \\[\n   f(x - f(a)) + a = f(x - f(b)) + b\n   \\]\n\n6. Since the terms \\( f(x - f(a)) \\) and \\( f(x - f(b)) \\) can be varied with \\( x \\), the only way for these to be true for all \\( x \\) is if \\( a = b \\), hence proving that \\( f \\) is injective.\n\n7. We next evaluate \\( P(x, -f(x)) \\):\n   \\[\n   f(2f(x)) = f(x - f(-f(x))) + f(x) - f(x)\n   \\]\n\n8. Simplify the expression:\n   \\[\n   f(2f(x)) = f(x - f(-f(x)))\n   \\]\n\n9. By injectivity, this implies:\n   \\[\n   x - f(-f(x)) = 2f(x)\n   \\]\n\n10. Isolating \\( f(-f(x)) \\), we get:\n    \\[\n    f(-f(x)) = x - 2f(x).\n    \\]\n\n11. Now consider \\( P(x, 0) \\):\n    \\[\n    f(2f(x)) = f(x - f(0)) + f(x) + 0\n    \\]\n\n12. Simplifying by using \\( P(x, 0) \\):\n    \\[\n    f(2f(x)) = f(x - f(0)) + f(x).\n    \\]\n\n13. Combining this with our initial result from \\( P(x, y) \\), we have:\n    \\[\n    f(x - f(0)) + f(x) = f(x - f(y)) + f(x) + y.\n    \\]\n\n14. Simplifying the above equation:\n    \\[\n    f(x - f(0)) = f(x - f(y)) + y.\n    \\]\n\n15. Let \\( x = 0 \\):\n    \\[\n    f(-f(0)) = f(-f(y)) + y.\n    \\]\n    \n16. By injectivity, consider setting \\( y = 0 \\):\n    \\[\n    f(-f(0)) = f(-f(0)) + 0.\n    \\]\n    \n17. Hence,\n    \\[\n    f(-f(0)) = f(-f(y)) + y \\Longrightarrow f(y) = y + c,\n    \\]\n    where \\( f \\)'s output is linear \\( y + c \\).\n\n18. Substitute \\( f(y) = y + c \\) into the original functional equation:\n    \\[\n    f(2f(x)) = f(x - f(y)) + f(x) + y \\Rightarrow 2(x + c) + c = x - (y + c) + x + c + y.\n    \\]\n\n19. Simplify the resulting equation:\n    \\[\n    2x + 3c = x - y + x + c + y + c \\Rightarrow 2x + 3c = 2x + 2c.\n    \\]\n\n20. Resolving this, we obtain:\n    \\[\n    3c = 2c \\Rightarrow c = 0.\n    \\]\n\n21. Therefore, the function is:\n    \\[\n    f(x) = x.\n    \\]\n   \n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(x) = x}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}]